    <string name="diag_notice_local_vs_public">These are the addresses of your device on its own network. Private, link-local and carrier-grade NAT (100.64.0.0/10) addresses are only valid inside your local or provider network – they are not your public internet address. Your public IP is the address remote servers see; the IP Info website shows it.</string>
    <string name="diag_action_public_ip">Show my public IP</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_wifi_permission">Android only reveals the Wi-Fi name (SSID) and access point (BSSID) to apps with the precise location permission, and only while location is turned on. IP Info reads just the network you’re connected to – it doesn’t scan for nearby networks, and the values stay on this device.</string>
    <string name="diag_action_allow_wifi_name">Allow Wi-Fi name access</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_wifi_permission_denied">Wi-Fi name access was declined and Android won’t ask again. Everything else on this screen still works. You can allow the precise location permission in the app settings.</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_wifi_approximate">Only approximate location is allowed. Android needs precise location to reveal the Wi-Fi name.</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_wifi_location_off">Location services are turned off. Android hides the Wi-Fi name while location is off.</string>
    <string name="diag_action_open_location_settings">Open location settings</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_vpn_unknown">A VPN is active, but its app didn’t tell Android which network it runs over, so the underlying connection can’t be determined with certainty.</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_vpn_wifi">A Wi-Fi network is connected, but Android doesn’t confirm that VPN traffic uses it.</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_no_network">No network is connected. Native diagnostics keep working; the website’s online tools need a connection.</string>
    <string name="diag_notice_privacy">These details stay on this device: they’re read from Android on demand, aren’t stored and are never sent to the IP Info backend or anyone else.</string>
    <string name="diag_permission_title">Show the Wi-Fi name?</string>
    <string name="diag_permission_message">To display the Wi-Fi name and access point, Android requires the location permission. IP Info uses it only for this screen, doesn’t scan for nearby networks, and keeps the result on your device. You can still use all other diagnostics without it.</string>
